{% doc %}
  Turns one posted inbound email into a ticket and a message.

  Provider agnostic on purpose: it takes already extracted fields, so a
  second provider needs its own endpoint page to map the post into these
  names, and nothing in here changes.

  Returns a result with an HTTP status for the endpoint to apply.

  @param {object} params - from, to, cc, subject, text, html, headers, token
{% enddoc %}
{% liquid
  function settings = 'modules/support/queries/settings/get'
  if settings == blank
    assign result = { "ok": false, "error": "not_configured", "status": 500 }
    return result
  endif

  comment
    The shared secret. SendGrid Inbound Parse does not sign its posts the
    way the Event Webhook signs its own, so the secret travels in the URL,
    as a path segment or a query parameter.
  endcomment
  assign given = params.token | default: ""
  if settings.webhook_secret == blank or given != settings.webhook_secret
    assign result = { "ok": false, "error": "forbidden", "status": 403 }
    return result
  endif

  comment
    What the provider does with each answer, because it decides which
    failures are recoverable. SendGrid Inbound Parse retries a 5xx for up
    to three days and then drops the email with no bounce to the sender
    and no alert anywhere. A 4xx it treats as final, so the email is gone
    at once and just as quietly. There is therefore no status code that
    means "tell someone": a refusal here either gets retried or vanishes.
    So anything an install can fix answers 5xx, and anything no retry
    could ever change answers 200 and is logged.
  endcomment
  function sender = 'modules/support/helpers/email/parse_address', raw: params.from
  if sender.email == blank
    comment
      No parseable From. Retrying cannot conjure one, and with no address
      there is nobody to reply to even if an agent wanted to, so this is
      as far as the email goes. Logged, because an address we cannot parse
      and should be able to is a bug in the parser.
    endcomment
    log params.from, type: 'modules/support/commands/inbound/receive: dropped, no sender address'
    assign result = { "ok": false, "error": "no_sender", "status": 200 }
    return result
  endif

  comment
    A blocked sender is refused before anything is written, and answered
    200 because the refusal is permanent and deliberate. A 5xx here would
    have the provider deliver the same blocked email every few hours for
    three days.
  endcomment
  function blocked = 'modules/support/queries/blocked_senders/find_match', email: sender.email
  if blocked
    log sender.email, type: 'modules/support/commands/inbound/receive: refused, sender is blocked'
    assign result = { "ok": true, "status": 200, "blocked": true, "blocked_by_rule": blocked.value }
    return result
  endif

  function headers = 'modules/support/helpers/email/parse_headers', raw: params.headers

  comment
    The same email twice. A provider retries when it did not hear a 2xx,
    and "did not hear" includes the case where everything below ran and
    committed and only the response was lost. Without this guard that
    retry opens a second ticket for one customer email, and the customer
    gets two receipts.

    A Message-ID is globally unique and assigned by the sender's own
    client, so having stored one already is proof we have seen this email.
    Mail with no Message-ID at all cannot be deduplicated and falls
    through, which is the same position we were in before.
  endcomment
  if headers.message_id != blank
    assign seen_ids = headers.message_id | split: ','
    function already = 'modules/support/queries/messages/find_by_header_ids', message_ids: seen_ids
    if already
      function seen_ticket = 'modules/support/queries/tickets/find', id: already.ticket_id, number: null
      log headers.message_id, type: 'modules/support/commands/inbound/receive: already had this Message-ID, nothing written'
      assign result = { "ok": true, "status": 200, "duplicate": true, "ticket_id": already.ticket_id, "ticket_number": seen_ticket.number, "message_id": already.id }
      return result
    endif
  endif

  function to_addresses = 'modules/support/helpers/email/parse_address_list', raw: params.to
  function cc_addresses = 'modules/support/helpers/email/parse_address_list', raw: params.cc
  assign recipients = to_addresses | concat: cc_addresses

  assign department = null
  for address in recipients
    function match = 'modules/support/queries/departments/find_by_inbound_address', address: address
    if match
      assign department = match
      break
    endif
  endfor

  assign routed = true
  if department == blank
    comment
      Nothing matched the recipient. Fall back to the first department
      rather than drop the mail: a misaddressed email is a support request
      that has not been answered yet, and an agent can move it.
    endcomment
    function department = 'modules/support/queries/departments/default'
    assign routed = false
  endif

  if department == blank
    assign result = { "ok": false, "error": "no_department", "status": 500 }
    return result
  endif

  function contact = 'modules/support/commands/contacts/find_or_create', email: sender.email, name: sender.name, organization: null
  unless contact.valid
    comment
      The address parsed and then would not validate, or the write
      failed. Either way this is ours to fix, not the sender's, so answer
      5xx and let the provider bring the email back for three days while
      someone does.
    endcomment
    log contact.errors, type: 'ERROR: modules/support/commands/inbound/receive could not record the contact'
    assign result = { "ok": false, "error": "contact_invalid", "status": 500 }
    return result
  endunless

  comment
    A sender hammering the address is a loop, a broken integration or an
    attack, and all three want the same answer. Twenty an hour is far
    above anything a person types and far below what it takes to fill a
    queue. Over it, the mail is dropped and logged rather than stored:
    recording ten thousand copies of the same email is worse than losing
    the ten thousand and first.
  endcomment
  assign inbound_per_hour = 20
  assign hour_ago = 'now' | date: '%s' | minus: 3600 | date: '%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S'
  function recent = 'modules/support/queries/messages/count_from_contact', contact_id: contact.id, since: hour_ago
  if recent >= inbound_per_hour
    log sender.email, type: 'modules/support/commands/inbound/receive: suspected loop, over the hourly limit for this sender'
    assign result = { "ok": true, "status": 200, "suspected_loop": true, "recent_messages": recent }
    return result
  endif

  comment
    Whether anything may be sent back. Worked out here, where the headers
    are, and carried on the event, because the consumer that would send
    the receipt has no email in front of it.
  endcomment
  function automation = 'modules/support/helpers/email/is_automated', headers: headers, address: sender.email
  comment
    Copy the references rather than aliasing them. add_to_array mutates the
    array it is given, so appending to headers.references here would also
    append to what gets stored on the message.
  endcomment
  assign candidates = []
  for reference in headers.references
    assign candidates = candidates | array_add: reference
  endfor
  if headers.in_reply_to != blank
    assign candidates = candidates | array_add: headers.in_reply_to
  endif

  assign ticket = null
  assign matched_by = "none"

  if candidates != blank
    function prior = 'modules/support/queries/messages/find_by_header_ids', message_ids: candidates
    if prior
      function ticket = 'modules/support/queries/tickets/find', id: prior.ticket_id, number: null
      if ticket
        assign matched_by = "headers"
      endif
    endif
  endif

  if ticket == blank
    comment
      The fallback for clients that drop In-Reply-To and References: the
      [#18022] token outbound replies carry in their subject.

      Unlike a Message-ID, a ticket number is short, sequential and
      printed in every email we send, so anyone who has ever been copied
      on one can guess the rest. Matching on it alone would let a stranger
      post into any conversation by putting the right number in a subject
      line, and the message would appear in the thread looking exactly
      like the customer wrote it. So the number is only honoured for
      someone already on that ticket: the requester, at any of their
      addresses, or an address already copied in. Everyone else opens a
      new ticket, which is the safe outcome and a visible one.
    endcomment
    function number = 'modules/support/helpers/email/ticket_number_from_subject', subject: params.subject
    if number
      function candidate = 'modules/support/queries/tickets/find', id: null, number: number
      if candidate
        function belongs = 'modules/support/helpers/email/is_on_ticket', ticket: candidate, email: sender.email
        if belongs
          assign ticket = candidate
          assign matched_by = "subject"
        else
          log sender.email, type: 'modules/support/commands/inbound/receive: subject named a ticket this sender is not on, opening a new one'
        endif
      endif
    endif
  endif

  function split = 'modules/support/helpers/email/split_quoted', text: params.text, html: params.html
  assign quoted = split.quoted_html
  if quoted == blank
    assign quoted = split.quoted_text
  endif

  comment
    Everything from here writes, and the writes only make sense together.
    A ticket with no message on it is a customer who wrote in and whose
    email vanished, and nothing downstream would ever notice: it would sit
    in the queue looking like an empty conversation. So they go in one
    transaction, and anything that fails part way takes the rest with it.

    The contact is deliberately outside it. Finding or creating one is
    idempotent on the address, and a contact with no ticket yet is not a
    broken state, it is just a contact.

    rollback does not hand control back: it raises out of the whole
    execution, so there is no code path after endtransaction that can turn
    a rolled back write into a tidy 422. The endpoint answers 5xx and the
    provider retries, which is the right answer to the only thing that can
    get this far. Everything a malformed email can break, a missing
    sender, an unroutable recipient, a contact that will not validate, is
    refused above with a 4xx before a single write happens. A failure
    inside this block means the install is wrong, most likely statuses
    that were never seeded, and a retry after someone fixes it will work.
  endcomment
  assign is_new = false
  assign reopened = false
  assign attachments_saved = 0

  transaction
    if ticket == blank
      assign is_new = true
      function ticket = 'modules/support/commands/tickets/create', subject: params.subject, department_id: department.id, contact_id: contact.id, contact_email: contact.email, settings: settings
      unless ticket.valid
        log ticket.errors, type: 'ERROR: modules/support/commands/inbound/receive could not open the ticket'
        rollback
      endunless
    endif

    comment
      Why this message landed here, kept on the message rather than only
      returned to the provider. When a reply turns up on the wrong ticket,
      this and the header block below are the whole investigation: without
      them the answer is a shrug, because the email itself is gone.
    endcomment
    assign match_reason = matched_by
    if is_new
      assign match_reason = 'new'
    endif

    assign incoming = { "ticket_id": ticket.id, "kind": "inbound", "contact_id": contact.id, "user_id": null, "from_address": sender.email, "to_addresses": to_addresses, "cc_addresses": cc_addresses, "subject": params.subject, "body_html": split.body_html, "body_text": split.body_text, "quoted_html": quoted, "message_id_header": headers.message_id, "in_reply_to": headers.in_reply_to, "references": headers.references, "provider_message_id": null, "delivery_status": null, "match_reason": match_reason, "raw_headers": params.headers }
    function message = 'modules/support/commands/messages/create', object: incoming
    unless message.valid
      log message.errors, type: 'ERROR: modules/support/commands/inbound/receive could not record the message'
      rollback
    endunless

    comment
      Attachments now arrive already taken apart, because Inbound Parse
      runs in raw mode and the endpoint hands the whole message to the
      MIME parser before anything gets here. Each entry carries its name,
      type, size, inline flag and its bytes.

      Only the metadata is written for now. Storing the bytes needs the
      presigned upload dance, which is its own piece of work; the parser
      already carries them, so that is an addition rather than a rewrite.
    endcomment
    for file in params.attachments_list
      assign attachment = { "message_id": message.id, "filename": file.filename, "content_type": file.content_type, "size": file.size }
      function saved = 'modules/support/commands/attachments/create', object: attachment
      if saved.valid
        assign attachments_saved = attachments_saved | plus: 1
      endif
    endfor

    comment
      Who else is on this conversation now. The sender goes in along with
      the addresses they copied, and the helper drops them again if they
      are the requester, which they are on every ticket but one: a reply
      that threaded by header from an address we do not know. That is a
      colleague joining the thread, not the customer changing address, so
      they become a CC and the ticket keeps its requester.

      The requester is the person who opened the ticket, who is only the
      sender of this particular email on a new ticket. Passing the sender
      instead excludes whoever just wrote in, which drops the colleague
      this is meant to add and leaves the actual requester free to end up
      copied on their own conversation.
    endcomment
    assign requester = contact
    unless is_new
      function requester = 'modules/support/queries/contacts/find', id: ticket.contact_id
    endunless

    assign joining = []
    for address in cc_addresses
      assign joining = joining | array_add: address
    endfor
    assign joining = joining | array_add: sender.email
    function thread_cc = 'modules/support/helpers/email/thread_ccs', existing: ticket.cc_addresses, department: department, contact: requester, incoming: joining
    assign previous_cc = ticket.cc_addresses | join: ','
    assign merged_cc = thread_cc | join: ','
    if merged_cc != previous_cc
      graphql _ = 'modules/support/tickets/set_cc', id: ticket.id, cc_addresses: thread_cc
    endif

    unless is_new
      function status = 'modules/support/queries/statuses/find', id: ticket.status_id
      comment
        Spam is closed like any other finished status, and unlike any
        other finished status it must stay shut. Reopening it would mean
        one unblocked address could walk a spam thread back into the
        queue by replying to it.
      endcomment
      if status.kind == 'closed' and status.key != 'spam'
        function _ = 'modules/support/commands/tickets/reopen', ticket: ticket
        assign reopened = true
        assign reopened_from = status.name
      endif
    endunless

    function _ = 'modules/support/commands/tickets/touch', ticket: ticket, direction: 'inbound', contact_email: contact.email
  endtransaction

  comment
    Published after the transaction commits, never from inside it. A
    consumer runs in the background, and a background job woken by a write
    that then rolled back would be chasing a ticket that does not exist.

    There are no consumers yet. That is the point of publishing now: the
    notifications Phase 4 needs can be added as consumers without anyone
    touching the write path again.
  endcomment
  if is_new
    assign created_payload = { "ticket_id": ticket.id, "number": ticket.number, "department_id": department.id, "contact_id": contact.id, "priority": ticket.priority, "automated": automation.automated, "automated_reason": automation.reason }
    function _ = 'modules/core/commands/events/publish', type: 'ticket_created', object: created_payload, delay: null, max_attempts: null
  endif

  assign received_payload = { "ticket_id": ticket.id, "message_id": message.id, "contact_id": contact.id, "new_ticket": is_new }
  function _ = 'modules/core/commands/events/publish', type: 'message_received', object: received_payload, delay: null, max_attempts: null

  if reopened
    function reopen_target = 'modules/support/queries/statuses/flagged', flag: 'is_reopen_target'
    assign reopened_payload = { "ticket_id": ticket.id, "from_status": reopened_from, "to_status": reopen_target.name }
    function _ = 'modules/core/commands/events/publish', type: 'ticket_reopened', object: reopened_payload, delay: null, max_attempts: null
  endif

  assign result = { "ok": true, "status": 200, "ticket_id": ticket.id, "ticket_number": ticket.number, "message_id": message.id, "new_ticket": is_new, "reopened": reopened, "matched_by": match_reason, "attachments": attachments_saved, "department": department.slug, "routed_by_address": routed, "automated": automation.automated, "automated_reason": automation.reason }
  return result
%}
